      "content": "<h3>What is a LinkedHashMap?</h3>\n<p>A <strong>LinkedHashMap</strong> is a <code>HashMap</code> that <strong>remembers the order of its entries</strong>. Everything you know about HashMap still applies (unique keys, O(1) lookups, one null key), but when you print or loop over it, the pairs come out in a <strong>predictable order</strong>: the order in which the keys were first added.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Package <code>java.util</code>. It <strong>extends HashMap</strong> and (Java 21) implements <code>SequencedMap</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Insertion order (default):</strong> iteration follows the order of first insertion. Putting an existing key again does <strong>not</strong> move it.</li>\n<li><strong>Access order (optional):</strong> with the constructor flag <code>accessOrder = true</code> the most recently <strong>read or written</strong> entry moves to the end. This is the basis of an <strong>LRU cache</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fast:</strong> get, put and remove are O(1) on average, with a small extra cost to keep the links.</li>\n<li>One null key and null values are allowed. <strong>Not thread-safe.</strong></li>\n<li>Use it for: reports and JSON output in a stable order, \"last seen\" lists, LRU caches.</li>\n</ul>"
